43:19.220 --> 43:22.560
Number two, where is your code that you actually talked about

43:22.560 --> 43:25.640
that works for doing the switch route thing

43:25.640 --> 43:27.420
so you don't get the dependency problem?

43:30.080 --> 43:31.420
Well, it's a bit different.

43:35.580 --> 43:38.740
The problem with the dependency loop is within Yocto.

43:38.740 --> 43:43.200
It does not have something to do with the final root of S.

43:43.200 --> 43:46.280
So it's only when you actually build the root of S

43:46.280 --> 43:56.480
is the problem. Well, it's a new, well, recipe, so that's how Yocto work. So the question

43:56.480 --> 44:06.280
is, is the recipe in Yocto yet? No. It's, well, in our Yocto, yeah. Yeah, no. But, well,

44:06.280 --> 44:18.020
definitely would want to work on it so I have two two questions to firstly how

44:18.020 --> 44:23.860
many boards do you actually break doing this and secondly is it emulator ball

44:23.860 --> 44:31.660
and sound like you emu so good question the first one of how many boards are we

44:31.660 --> 44:40.960
broken yet, so none. Yeah, there are a lot of tutorials on the internet, so luckily,

44:40.960 --> 44:47.840
we didn't have to break any boards, so, well, we're happy with this. And the next question

44:47.840 --> 45:06.560
was QMUs support I haven't yeah I don't know sorry you mentioned the performance

45:06.560 --> 45:13.740
impact of the verification? Did you actually measure how much it affected the boot time

45:13.740 --> 45:21.740
in your case? So I think there's not really a point

45:21.740 --> 45:31.760
measuring it because if you need a secure device, well, the latency, you don't care really.

45:31.760 --> 45:38.600
On the rootFS part, they pretend that the IO takes so much time compared to the hash

45:38.600 --> 45:47.640
computation that, well, you can ignore it.

45:47.640 --> 45:53.540
Any reason you do verification of your rootFS and kernel and not U-boot?

45:53.540 --> 46:01.760
So for example, can U-boot read this block device?

46:01.760 --> 46:08.380
So the question is where the verification of the rootfs is.

46:08.380 --> 46:09.520
Why not in your boot?

46:09.520 --> 46:12.340
There is no support for SquashFS, first.

46:12.340 --> 46:19.440
And second is that, oh, that was it.

46:19.440 --> 46:24.260
It's on two different mediums for media for our clients.

46:24.260 --> 46:28.700
So the first one is used to store the kernel, et cetera.

46:28.700 --> 46:42.080
And the rootFS is on another medium.

46:42.080 --> 46:47.840
So there is a really interesting remarks, and I forgot about it.

46:47.840 --> 46:53.220
It's actually done only on the read access of your data.

46:53.220 --> 46:58.700
So you cannot actually use the bootloader to verify your rootFS because it's done only

46:58.700 --> 47:02.700
when you access data, which is done only by the kernel.

47:02.700 --> 47:06.700
Yep.

47:06.700 --> 47:10.700
You have four public keys stored in your boot image.

47:10.700 --> 47:14.700
How do you revoke one if one's bad?

47:14.700 --> 47:18.700
The same way you actually flash the public key, so you blow a fuse.

47:18.700 --> 47:22.700
And this fuse just say, well, this one is revoked.

47:22.700 --> 47:26.700
That's it.

47:26.700 --> 47:29.740
Oh, from U-boot.

47:29.740 --> 47:33.880
So within U-boot, with your update scripts or whatever,

47:33.880 --> 47:38.580
you ask it to blow the fuse.

47:38.580 --> 47:41.640
But yeah, if it's already too late, then I'll be screwed.

47:46.220 --> 47:47.220
Anyone?

47:47.220 --> 47:47.720
No.

47:47.720 --> 47:48.720
